  "response": "The supplied snapshot does not show an additional test that combines `OSError` from `seek` with a partially consumed body. `test_rewind_body_failed_seek` is located in `tests/test_requests.py`, and its `BadFileObj.seek()` always raises `OSError`, but the body is newly prepared at position `0`; it is not partially read first [Data: Sources (94); Entities (1308, 1301)].\n\nThere is a separate partial-consumption test, `test_rewind_partially_read_body`, in `tests/test_requests.py`. It reads four bytes before preparation, verifies `_body_position == 4`, exhausts the remaining body, and successfully rewinds it, but it uses `io.BytesIO`, so it does not exercise a failing `seek` [Data: Sources (94); Entities (1299)].\n\nThere is also `test_manual_redirect_with_partial_body_read` in `tests/test_requests.py`, which partially reads one redirect response and fully reads another while following multiple redirects. However, it checks redirect status transitions only and does not use a body whose `seek` raises `OSError` [Data: Sources (94); Entities (1296)]. Based on this snapshot, coverage for the combined cases—failed `seek` after partial body consumption and failed `seek` during or after multiple redirects—appears insufficient. A focused change would be to add tests that combine the `BadFileObj` failing `seek` behavior with the partial-read and multi-redirect setups; this is a proposed change, not an existing test shown in the evidence.",
  "score": 94,
  "follow_up_queries": [
    "Which redirect code path invokes `rewind_body`, and what preparation state does it require after a partially consumed streamed body?",
    "Are there existing tests for `UnrewindableBodyError` when a redirected POST body fails to rewind after more than one redirect?"
  ]
}
